  "emoji": "🤔",
  "summary": "Make 'if' sentences: إِذَا + a past verb for things that can really happen, إِنْ in إِنْ شَاءَ اللّٰهُ, and لَوْ … la- for dreams and things that are not real.",
  "intro": "Arabic has three words for 'if'. إِذَا is for things that can really happen: إِذَا أَمْطَرَتْ، فَسَنَزُورُ الْمَتْحَفَ (if it rains, we'll visit the museum). The verb after إِذَا is in the past, but the meaning is the future. إِنْ means the same and is a little more formal; you hear it every day in إِنْ شَاءَ اللّٰهُ (God willing). لَوْ is for things that are not real: لَوْ كَانَ عِنْدِي مَالٌ كَثِيرٌ، لَسَافَرْتُ حَوْلَ الْعَالَمِ (if I had a lot of money, I would travel around the world). The answer after لَوْ starts with la- and a past verb.",
  "objectives": [
    "Talk about real plans with إِذَا + a past verb: إِذَا أَمْطَرَتْ، فَسَنَزُورُ الْمَتْحَفَ",
    "Imagine and dream with لَوْ … la-: لَوْ كُنْتُ غَنِيًّا، لَاشْتَرَيْتُ بَيْتًا",
    "Use إِنْ شَاءَ اللّٰهُ and إِنْ + a past verb for the future",
    "Use the words for money, rich, dream, beach, a million and to win"
  ],
